---
import SomiBase from '../../layouts/SomiBase.astro'
import DocHeader from '../../components/somi/DocHeader.astro'
import { COMPANY, CONTACT, LEGAL_UPDATED, p } from '../../somi/config.js'
---

<SomiBase
  title="Health Disclaimer"
  variant="document"
  description="What somatic practice can and cannot do, and when to talk to a professional before using SoMi."
>
  <DocHeader
    eyebrow="Legal"
    title="Health Disclaimer"
    lede="Somatic work is gentle, but it is not nothing. Here is the honest version of what SoMi is for and who should check with someone first."
    updated={LEGAL_UPDATED}
  />

  <article class="prose-somi mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-28 pt-4 sm:px-8">
    <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl border border-teal/30 bg-teal/8 px-6 py-5">
      <p class="!mt-0 !mb-0 text-foam">
        <strong>If you are in crisis, SoMi is not the right tool.</strong> Call
        your local emergency number, or in the United States call or text
        <strong>988</strong> for the Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline. The app does
        not monitor what you write and nobody is reading it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="not-medical">SoMi is not medical care</h2>
    <p>
      SoMi is a general wellbeing and self-regulation app. It is <strong
        >not a medical device</strong
      >, not therapy, and not a substitute for diagnosis or treatment by a
      qualified professional. Nothing in the app or on this website is medical,
      psychological or psychiatric advice, and using SoMi does not create a
      clinical relationship of any kind.
    </p>
    <p>
      Never disregard professional advice or delay seeking it because of
      something you encountered in SoMi.
    </p>

    <h2 id="polyvagal">About the polyvagal framing</h2>
    <p>
      SoMi organises its exercises using a polyvagal-informed model — the energy
      and safety axes, and the state names like Steady, Wired and Shutdown. This
      is a <strong>useful way of sorting practices</strong>, and it is how many
      somatic practitioners work. It is not a measurement of your vagus nerve, it
      is not a diagnostic instrument, and the state the app shows you is derived
      entirely from where you placed your own finger on a picker. It reflects
      your self-report, nothing more.
    </p>
    <p>
      Aspects of polyvagal theory remain debated in the research literature. We
      present it as a practical organising lens, not settled clinical science.
    </p>

    <h2 id="check-first">Talk to a professional before starting if you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>are pregnant, or recently gave birth;</li>
      <li>
        have a heart condition, uncontrolled blood pressure, or a history of
        stroke;
      </li>
      <li>
        have a neck, spine, jaw or shoulder injury — several exercises involve
        head turns, gaze shifts and stretches;
      </li>
      <li>
        have an eye condition, retinal problem, glaucoma, or recent eye surgery —
        some exercises use sustained gaze direction;
      </li>
      <li>
        have an inner-ear or vestibular condition, or are prone to vertigo or
        fainting;
      </li>
      <li>have a seizure disorder;</li>
      <li>
        are being treated for a mental health condition, particularly trauma,
        PTSD, dissociation, psychosis, or an eating disorder;
      </li>
      <li>are recovering from surgery or an acute injury.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="what-can-happen">What can come up</h2>
    <p>
      Somatic practice deliberately brings attention into the body, and that can
      surface more than you expected. Sighing, yawning, shaking, trembling,
      tears, heat, tingling and spontaneous movement are all common and usually
      harmless — the app even gives you tags for them. Less often, turning
      attention inward can bring up difficult memories, strong emotion, numbness,
      or a feeling of being far away from yourself.
    </p>
    <p>
      If that happens: stop the flow, open your eyes, put your feet on the floor,
      and look around the room until it feels like a room again. If it keeps
      happening, work with a trauma-informed practitioner rather than alone with
      an app.
    </p>

    <h2 id="stop">Stop immediately if you experience</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>chest pain, pressure, or shortness of breath;</li>
      <li>dizziness, faintness, or visual disturbance;</li>
      <li>sharp or increasing pain anywhere;</li>
      <li>
        distress that keeps escalating rather than settling as the practice
        continues.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Then seek medical attention. Do not push through. Nothing in a five-minute
      flow is worth an injury.
    </p>

    <h2 id="practise-safely">Practising safely</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Work within a range that feels easy. Somatic practice is not a stretch
        you win.
      </li>
      <li>
        Never practise while driving, operating machinery, or anywhere you need
        your full attention.
      </li>
      <li>
        Do the lying-down exercises on a surface you can safely get down to and
        up from.
      </li>
      <li>
        Skip anything that does not feel right. Every block in a flow can be
        swapped or skipped.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="results">No promised results</h2>
    <p>
      We make no claim that SoMi will reduce your stress, improve your sleep,
      regulate your nervous system, or produce any other specific outcome.
      Individual responses to somatic practice vary widely, and streaks and stats
      in the app are a record of what you did, not evidence of a health benefit.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contact">Questions</h2>
    <p>
      This disclaimer forms part of our <a href={p('/terms')}>Terms of Use</a>. If
      something here is unclear, write to
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.support}`}>{CONTACT.support}</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      {COMPANY.name}<br />
      {COMPANY.addressLine}
    </p>
  </article>
</SomiBase>
